## Why This Is Asked
This probes governance at scale: per-tenant safety, data residency, and cost-aware routing in a live Azure OpenAI pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-aware policy packs in blob storage
- PDP (policy decision point) in Azure Functions
- Data residency routing to OpenAI regional endpoints
- Redaction and guardrails before outbound calls
- Auditing and observability via Azure Monitor

## Code Example
```javascript
function evaluatePDP(tenantId, metadata, policies) {
  const policy = policies[tenantId] || policies["default"];
  // residency check
  if (policy.residency && policy.residency !== metadata.region) return { action: "block" };
  // budget constraint
  if (typeof policy.budget === "number" && metadata.cost > policy.budget) return { action: "redirect", destination: "onPrem" };
  // allowed destination
  if (policy.allowedDestinations?.includes("OpenAI")) return { action: "allow", destination: "OpenAI" };
  return { action: "block" };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test PDP edge cases (missing tenant, malformed metadata, policy drift)?
- How would you implement zero-downtime policy updates and per-tenant rollbacks?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a secure, scalable OpenAI deployment with strict per-tenant isolation, governance, and rollout strategies in Azure.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant RBAC and per-tenant KBs in Cognitive Search
- Prompt-guard layer to prevent injection and data leakage
- PII redaction with Text Analytics
- Canary rollouts behind API Management
- Auditable logs to Data Lake and RBAC alignment via Azure AD

## Code Example
```javascript
// Guard layer sketch
async function guardPrompt(input, tenantId) {
  const policy = await kb.fetchTenantPolicy(tenantId);
  if (policy.blockList.some(r => input.includes(r))) {
    throw new Error('Input blocked by policy');
  }
  // optional rewriting rules
  return input;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would policy KB updates be rolled out without affecting active tenants?
- What are latency trade-offs and how would you mitigate them at scale?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests CDS governance, real-world guardrails, and Azure AI integration.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant prompts
- PII redaction and safety checks
- Governance metadata and provenance
- Audit logging to Data Lake
- Fallback policies and test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// example wrapper pseudocode for gating
async function respond(input, tenantId){
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  const ctx = await fetchContext(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(ctx, redacted);
  const risk = await safetyCheck(prompt);
  if(!risk.ok) return fallbackResponse(ctx);
  const res = await openaiChat(prompt);
  const final = attachGovernance(res, tenantId, ctx.modelVer);
  await log(final);
  return final;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the end-to-end logging and searchability of prompts? 
- How would you handle model version rollouts with zero-downtime for tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to translate regulatory requirements into automated, auditable technical controls using Azure AI and governance services, with strict multi-tenant isolation requirements.

## Key Concepts

- Regulatory-to-controls mapping (GDPR/CCPA/NIST)
- Knowledge base grounding with Cognitive Search
- Per-tenant RBAC and data isolation
- Automated evidence collection and auditable logging
- Azure Policy integration for continuous enforcement

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: Map obligations to controls and enforce per-tenant policy
async function ensureCompliance(clientId) {
  const obligations = await kb.fetchObligations(clientId);
  const controls = await mapToControls(obligations);

  const evidence = await collectEvidence(clientId, controls);
  const violations = await evaluateAgainstPolicy(controls, evidence);

  await logDecision({ clientId, obligations, controls, evidence, violations });
  return generateRemediationPlan(violations);
}
```
